---
/**
 * foodcraft / HeaderBM — THE STICKY WHITE RAIL. "Tahanan Cafe"'s front gate,
 * reproduced from the design's own <header>: a 96%-opaque WHITE bar over a
 * 12px backdrop blur, one --line hairline under it, and a single flex row at
 * 18px top and bottom carrying three things at a 22px gap — the 25px Libre
 * Caslon Text wordmark with its walnut town tag on the baseline beside it,
 * five 13.5px wayfinding links at a 27px gap, and the walnut rectangle (the
 * design's btnNav: the shared button at 13px/24px instead of 16px/30px).
 *
 * ROLE: wayfinding, plus the one action this page exists for. The button is a
 * SIBLING of the link row, never a child of it — the link row becomes a
 * display:none drawer at <=900px and the contact affordance must not leave
 * with it.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string on this rail, and nothing else:
 *   footer.brand                     — the wordmark (also the footer's; one
 *                                      field, two places, on purpose)
 *   footer.locality                  — the walnut town tag beside it
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label          — one nav link's label
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href           — that link's target (data-href-field)
 *   navCtaText                       — the walnut button's label
 *   navCtaHref                       — the button's target (data-href-field)
 * All six are declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts under the
 * `header` group, so every one is a hook a sidebar input actually owns. A
 * data-field on any other path would be a dead hook nothing can edit.
 *
 * DERIVED, never authored here:
 *   - the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when content.footer.brand
 *     is blank, because that is the same name from the same submission.
 *   - every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBM really
 *     renders (see the anchor guard below).
 *   - `i` is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index of a nav link, so dropping an
 *     unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *   - the nav rows are read from content.navbar_links, NOT layout.navLinks.
 *     An earlier draft of this file asserted that lib/astro-builder.ts "copies
 *     that same array onto layout.navLinks". It does not, and the claim was
 *     load-bearing: astro-builder.ts:880 resolves content.navbar_links to the
 *     owner's rows or else derive-content-defaults' FIVE, while :348 builds
 *     layout.navLinks from the RAW content.navbar_links or else FOUR hard-coded
 *     rows. On a site whose owner never touched the nav the two arrays are
 *     different lengths with different labels — so indexing layout.navLinks and
 *     writing navbar_links.${i} aimed the owner's edit at the wrong row
 *     (clicking the third cell, "Gallery", opened the input holding "Why us").
 *     The sidebar list edits navbar_links, so navbar_links.${i} is a live hook
 *     only when i indexes THAT array. layout.navLinks stays as a defensive
 *     fallback for a harness that renders this component without astro-builder.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "Tahanan", "San Juan", "Menu", "Our story",
 *     "Gallery", "Visit", "FAQ" and "Contact us" are the DESIGNER filling in
 *     their own template. Not one of those words is in this file. A café whose
 *     owner supplied no nav links gets no nav links, not the designer's five.
 *   - THE WHOLE RAIL, when brand, locality, links and button are all empty. A
 *     sticky bar with nothing in it is ~80px of dead chrome on every screen. It
 *     is the BODY content that is counted — never a tag or a headline, because
 *     lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills those on every site and a gate that
 *     counted them would always pass. (This rail has no tag and no headline at
 *     all: all four counted things are body content, which is why the count is
 *     simply everything the rail can draw.)
 *   - the burger, unless there is a drawer for it to open.
 *   - the wordmark, with no name. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real
 *     customer's own wordmark; with nothing supplied the town tag carries the
 *     lock alone, and with neither the lock is gone.
 *   - a nav link with no label, or whose href reaches no section on this page.
 *     A 0-width tap target and a link that scrolls nowhere are both broken.
 *   Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so a blank value removes the ELEMENT and
 *   not just its text — no empty walnut rectangle, no lock with nothing in it.
 *   There is no star row, rating or numeral on this rail, so nothing here can
 *   print a claim the owner did not make.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE READ AS OVERSIGHTS
 *
 * 1. THE TOWN TAG IS NOT A .kick, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN'S CALL. The signature
 *    of this template is the UNDERLINED eyebrow, and .kick carries that
 *    underline for every section. But the design's header tag is
 *    `font-size:9px;letter-spacing:.32em;uppercase;#7A4E2D;700` with NO
 *    border-bottom and no padding-bottom — same in the footer lock. Only the
 *    section eyebrows (hero, about, menu…) take the hairline. Putting .kick on
 *    the wordmark tag would draw a rule the design does not draw, floating
 *    under two words in the middle of a bar that already has a hairline of its
 *    own. So this one tag is a scoped class, at the design's exact metrics.
 *
 * 2. WHY THE TINT IS A PLAIN rgba() AND NOT A PSEUDO-ELEMENT. The design's rail
 *    is rgba(255,255,255,.96) — that is --paper at 96%. PageBM publishes
 *    --paper-rgb (and buildOverrideCss remaps it with the rest of the palette),
 *    so the design's exact value is expressible in one token declaration. No
 *    literal, no extra stacking context, same pixels.
 *
 * 3. WHY THE BUTTON HOOKS navCtaText AND NOT hero.cta1.text.
 *    components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle to a section by
 *    counting hooks of that prefix, so a header carrying hero.* hooks vanishes
 *    when the owner toggles the Hero block off. navCtaText/navCtaHref belong to
 *    the Header group, so this rail answers only to itself. footer.brand is
 *    safe twice over: the bridge only ever hides `closest('section,footer')`
 *    and a <header> is neither, and the real footer outweighs this file's two
 *    footer.* hooks many times over. The button does NOT fall back to the
 *    hero's label for its DISPLAY either — showing a string the Header-CTA box
 *    does not contain means the sidebar and the page disagree about what the
 *    button says. An empty box renders an empty rail, not the hero's words.
 *
 * 4. WHY THE NAV-LINK HOVER IS --accent-deep. It is the design's OWN hover: its
 *    stylesheet declares a:hover{color:#573418}, which is --accent-deep. In the
 *    demo that rule is shadowed by the inline colour on each nav link, so the
 *    designer's own page happens to render no hover change — an artefact of
 *    inline-style specificity, not a decision. Shipping a five-link row with no
 *    hover state would be an interaction regression dressed up as fidelity, so
 *    the design's stated hover colour is honoured (11.0:1 on white, over a rest
 *    colour of --ink-soft at 7.71:1, and it rides the same theme axis as every
 *    other walnut element on the page).
 *
 * 5. WHY THE BUTTON IS 44px AND THE RAIL IS ~80px, NOT THE DESIGN'S ~76px.
 *    btnNav declares no height and lands about 40px tall — under the 44px
 *    target size. Left alone it would be worse in the other direction: the
 *    wrapper's .btn carries min-height:48px and its own line-height:1, so the
 *    button would floor at 48px and give an 84px rail. So the
 *    button restates the design's 13px/24px padding, takes line-height:1.2
 *    (the `normal` the design actually renders at) and a 44px floor — the
 *    closest height to the design that is still a legal tap target. The
 *    wrapper's scroll-padding-top:88px clears the result, so in-page anchors
 *    still land below the rail.
 *
 * 6. WHY flex:none BECAME flex:0 1 auto ON THE LOCK. The design says flex:none,
 *    which at 360px lets a long single-word café name shove the button off
 *    screen. The lock shrinks and the wordmark ellipsises instead; at desktop
 *    widths the rendering is identical to the design.
 *
 * 7. THE BURGER AND DRAWER ARE AN ADDITION, NOT A RE-DERIVATION. The design is
 *    a desktop artboard and specifies no mobile rail at all. Five links at
 *    380px would push the page sideways, so the row collapses into a drawer at
 *    one width (<=900px) — the row leaves and the burger arrives together, so
 *    there is never a band where neither is on screen. Nothing in the desktop
 *    rendering moves. The drawer's ground is --paper, NOT --tint: --tint is
 *    invisible to the theme engine, so on a repainted scheme an off-white panel
 *    would hang over a page whose --paper had moved. The drawer is told apart
 *    from the page the way this design tells everything apart — by rules and a
 *    shadow, not by a fill.
 *
 * 8. target="_blank" ONLY FOR http(s). The design's CTA opens a messenger in a
 *    new tab. An owner may point this at #visit or a tel: instead, and
 *    hijacking those would be wrong, so the attribute pair is computed, never
 *    literal — and it is ONE conditional per attribute, so no attribute is ever
 *    emitted twice.
 *
 * 9. NO aria-label ON THE WORDMARK LOCK. Its accessible name is its own text:
 *    the owner's name and the owner's town. Appending a literal like "home"
 *    would announce a word this file never received. The two invisible strings
 *    that ARE written here — "Primary" on the nav landmark and "Menu" on the
 *    icon-only burger — are a11y affordances in the same class as the wrapper's
 *    skip link, never page copy. The burger's three bars are decorative and
 *    carry a literal aria-hidden; nothing on this rail carries a conditional
 *    one, so no element can end up with the attribute twice. There is no
 *    role="img" here: this rail holds no upload.
 *
 * 10. NO CLAMP ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE, AND NO TYPE SIZE THAT GROWS. Every size
 *     is a fixed px that only ever steps DOWN as the viewport narrows: 25→21
 *     wordmark, 12.5→10 button, and the nav link holds the design's 13.5px at
 *     EVERY width — the drawer restates only the ROW (48px min-height, 12/22px
 *     padding), never the type. An audit on the previous template found a
 *     headline that GREW past 520px because a floor in the 900 query sat under
 *     a cap in the 520 query; with no clamps and a ladder that is monotonic in
 *     one direction, that inversion is not expressible here.
 *
 * Never visibility-gated in this file: PageBM owns `visibility.headerSection`.
 * Buttons here are .btn + .btn--fill from the wrapper. Not .nav-cta, not
 * .btn-primary, not inside .nav-right — buildOverrideCss pins the label colour
 * on all three with !important, and `footer .btn` too, which is a further
 * reason this rail is a <header>.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX: every scoped class is `tcbm-`, unique to this template. An
 * audit had to rename a whole set of classes copied verbatim from the previous
 * template in this family; nothing here shares a name with HeaderBL's `hb-`.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const c = (content as any) || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes nav links to '#about', '#services',
// '#why', '#work', '#visit' and navCtaHref to '#visit', and lib/astro-builder.ts
// falls back to '#about', '#services', '#gallery', '#contact' — ids most designs
// never render, so those links scrolled nowhere. Resolve every in-page href
// against what PageBM ACTUALLY renders: keep it if the id exists, remap it when
// this design carries the same section under another name, otherwise fall back
// (nav links pass '' and are dropped). Off-page hrefs — tel:, mailto:, http(s)
// and anything else without a leading '#' — pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames four derived targets: the services block IS the menu
// (rooms lands there too), '#about' is the café's own story (host and why are
// the same block here), '#work' is the photo wall, and '#location' / '#contact'
// both mean the visit block — address, map and opening hours. A booking link
// has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. 'Your Business' was a shipped placeholder that rendered as the
// customer's own name — with nothing supplied the mark is omitted instead.
// `||`, never `??`: genericContentSchema gives footer.brand
// fallbackPaths ['business_name'] and ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks
// treats '' as empty, so a CLEARED brand box still shows the business name in
// the sidebar. With `??` an empty string is a value, and the page would have
// gone wordmark-less while the sidebar displayed a name — exactly the
// page/sidebar disagreement decision 3 above refuses for the button.
const brand = String(c.footer?.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// The town tag. One field, three consumers (here, the footer wordmark and the
// map caption) and nothing derives it, so on a first build it is simply absent
// rather than guessed out of the address.
const locality = String(c.footer?.locality ?? '').trim();

// Nav links. content.navbar_links is the array the sidebar list edits, so it is
// the array `i` must index — see the note above about layout.navLinks holding a
// DIFFERENT list on a site whose owner supplied no rows. `i` is the ORIGINAL
// index so per-link edits stay aimed at the row the owner clicked even after
// unreachable rows are dropped.
const navSource: any[] = Array.isArray(c.navbar_links)
  ? c.navbar_links
  : (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : []);
const navLinks: Array<{ href: string; label: string; i: number }> = navSource
  .map((l: any, i: number) => ({ label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(), href: onPage(l?.href, ''), i }))
  .filter((l: { href: string; label: string }) => l.href && l.label);

// The walnut button. Label and target both come from the Header group; an empty
// label removes the whole element rather than leaving a walnut rectangle.
const ctaText = String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
// '#visit' is the fallback because that is where this design puts the address,
// the map and the opening hours — the page has no checkout to send a booking
// link to, and it is the id the derived default already names.
const ctaHref = onPage(c.navCtaHref, '#visit');
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// The rail renders only when it CARRIES something — the lock, the links or the
// button. All four counted values are body content; there is no tag or headline
// on this rail for a derived default to fake the gate with.
const hasRail = Boolean(brand || locality || navLinks.length > 0 || ctaText);
---
{hasRail && (
  <header class="tcbm-rail">
    <div class="wrap tcbm-in">
      {(brand || locality) && (
        <a class="tcbm-lock" href="#main">
          {brand && <span class="tcbm-mark" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
          {locality && <span class="tcbm-town" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
        </a>
      )}
      {navLinks.length > 0 && (
        <nav class="tcbm-nav" id="tcbm-nav-BM" aria-label="Primary">
          {navLinks.map((link) => (
            <a
              href={link.href}
              data-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.label`}
              data-href-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.href`}
            >{link.label}</a>
          ))}
        </nav>
      )}
      {/* OUTSIDE .tcbm-nav on purpose: .tcbm-nav is a display:none drawer at
          <=900px and the one action this template exists for must survive it. */}
      {ctaText && (
        <a
          href={ctaHref}
          class="btn btn--fill tcbm-cta"
          target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
          rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
          data-field="navCtaText"
          data-href-field="navCtaHref"
        >{ctaText}</a>
      )}
      {navLinks.length > 0 && (
        <button class="tcbm-burger" type="button" aria-label="Menu" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="tcbm-nav-BM">
          <span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
      )}
    </div>
  </header>
)}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    // Null-guarded rather than conditionally rendered: an is:inline script must
    // stay at the top level, and with no rail (or no drawer) it simply exits.
    // 'tcbm-rail' is template-unique because a preview harness can render two
    // wrappers into one document and a shared class cross-wires the drawers.
    var rail = document.querySelector('header.tcbm-rail');
    if (!rail) return;
    var btn = rail.querySelector('.tcbm-burger');
    if (!btn) return;
    function setOpen(open) {
      rail.classList.toggle('is-open', open);
      btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
    }
    btn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!rail.classList.contains('is-open')); });
    // '.tcbm-cta' is in this selector because the button lives OUTSIDE the
    // drawer: tapping it while the drawer is open must still close the drawer.
    rail.querySelectorAll('.tcbm-nav a, .tcbm-cta').forEach(function (a) {
      a.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
    });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) { if (e.key === 'Escape') setOpen(false); });
  })();
</script>
<style>
  /* The design: sticky, 96% white, blur(12px), one --line hairline under it.
     The tint is a straight rgba() on --paper-rgb, which PageBM publishes and
     the theme engine remaps with the rest of the palette (see note 2 in the
     frontmatter) — so this is the design's own rgba(255,255,255,.96) with no
     literal in it.
     (Never write a literal end-style tag inside this block, comments included:
      it is an HTML raw-text element and the parser would end the stylesheet
      right there, silently truncating the file.) */
  .tcbm-rail {
    /* z-index is the design's own 50. The wrapper's skip link sits at 100, so
       the rail clears the page and the skip link still clears the rail. */
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 50;
    background-color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .96);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
  }

  /* The design's row: 18px top and bottom, 22px gap, three parts spread apart.
     Its 30px side padding is the .wrap's job in this family (min(1160px,92vw)),
     so only the vertical value is restated here.
     NOT position:relative — the <=900px drawer is absolutely positioned and
     resolves against .tcbm-rail (position:sticky is a positioned element) so it
     spans the full rail width rather than the wrap.
     justify-content is flex-end rather than the design's space-between because
     the auto margins below already reproduce space-between whenever two or more
     parts are in flow (an auto margin eats the free space before
     justify-content ever sees it). It only takes effect in the degenerate cases
     the design never drew: a café with no brand and no town tag, where at
     <=900px the drawer has left the flow and the button and burger would
     otherwise sit hard LEFT. */
  .tcbm-in { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: flex-end; gap: 22px; padding: 18px 0; }

  /* Wordmark + town tag share a baseline, exactly as the design draws them at
     an 11px gap. Both the lock and the link row take an auto right margin: with
     all three present that reproduces the design's space-between, and with
     either one missing the button still sits hard right. */
  .tcbm-lock {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 11px;
    margin-right: auto; flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .tcbm-mark {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 25px;
    letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--ink);
    /* line-height is the DESIGN'S value: it states none, so its wordmark
       renders at `normal`. It is RESTATED rather than left to inherit because
       the wrapper's body sets 1.78 and 25px of Libre Caslon Text would sit in
       a 44px line box. It must not be TIGHTENED either: `normal` is the face's
       own ascent+descent, and any smaller line box is clipped by the
       overflow:hidden that the ellipsis below requires — shaving the tail off
       a descender (Tahanan, Kape) and the tilde off a capital (Niño). */
    line-height: normal;
    /* A long single-word café name must not push the button off a 360px
       screen; flex items are blockified, so ellipsis applies here. */
    min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  /* NOT .kick — see note 1. The design gives this tag no underline: 9px caps at
     .32em in walnut, weight 700, and nothing else. */
  .tcbm-town {
    flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 700; font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: .32em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }

  .tcbm-nav { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 27px; margin-right: auto; }
  .tcbm-nav a {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 400; font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    transition: color .18s ease;
  }
  /* The design's own hover, straight from its stylesheet: #573418, which is
     --accent-deep — see note 4 in the frontmatter. Same theme axis as --accent,
     over a rest colour of --ink-soft. */
  .tcbm-nav a:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .tcbm-nav a { transition: none; } }

  /* The design's btnNav is the wrapper's .btn--fill at a smaller size: walnut
     fill, --on-accent label, 2px corner, 12.5px/.14em caps, 13px/24px padding.
     Only the METRICS are restated — no fill, no label colour, no box-shadow —
     so the wrapper's hover darkening and its focus ring both stay intact.
     line-height and min-height are the two values that are not verbatim; note 5
     in the frontmatter says why 44px is the honest number. */
  .tcbm-cta { flex: none; padding: 13px 24px; line-height: 1.2; min-height: 44px; }

  /* The design specifies no mobile rail (note 7). The burger borrows the
     design's OWN secondary-control treatment — no fill inside a --line-2
     hairline at a 2px corner, which is exactly its outlined button — so the
     addition stays inside the template's vocabulary. */
  .tcbm-burger {
    display: none; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; gap: 5px;
    width: 46px; height: 44px; padding: 11px 10px; flex: none;
    background-color: transparent; border: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    border-radius: var(--r); cursor: pointer;
  }
  .tcbm-burger span {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: 2px; background-color: var(--ink);
    transition: transform .2s ease, opacity .2s ease;
  }
  header.tcbm-rail.is-open .tcbm-burger span:nth-child(1) { transform: translateY(7px) rotate(45deg); }
  header.tcbm-rail.is-open .tcbm-burger span:nth-child(2) { opacity: 0; }
  header.tcbm-rail.is-open .tcbm-burger span:nth-child(3) { transform: translateY(-7px) rotate(-45deg); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .tcbm-burger span { transition: none; } }

  /* ONE collapse width: the row leaves and the burger arrives together, so
     there is never a band where neither is on screen. The drawer is OPAQUE
     --paper rather than the rail's 96% white — it hangs over live page content
     and a translucent panel would print the page through the links — and it is
     separated from the page by rules and a shadow rather than by a fill, which
     is how this design draws every other boundary on it. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .tcbm-burger { display: flex; }
    .tcbm-nav {
      position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: 100%;
      display: none; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: 0;
      background-color: var(--paper);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-2);
      box-shadow: 0 14px 30px rgba(var(--ink-rgb), .12);
      padding: 4px 0 10px; max-height: 78vh; overflow-y: auto;
    }
    header.tcbm-rail.is-open .tcbm-nav { display: flex; }
    /* The ROW becomes a 48px target; the TYPE stays at the design's 13.5px.
       13.5px at the inherited line-height 1.78 is a 24px line box, so 12px of
       padding clears 48px on its own — the target is bought with the row, never
       by growing type as the viewport narrows (note 10). */
    .tcbm-nav a {
      display: flex; align-items: center; min-height: 48px; padding: 12px 22px;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .tcbm-in { gap: 10px; padding: 14px 0; }
    .tcbm-mark { font-size: 21px; }
    .tcbm-town { letter-spacing: .24em; }
    /* display:block, not inline-flex, so text-overflow actually applies; a long
       owner-written label is ellipsised rather than widening the rail past the
       viewport. 12px padding over a 20px line box still clears a 44px target.
       The wrapper's 520 pass also drops .btn padding to 15px 22px, so the
       padding is restated here to keep the design's tighter nav metric. */
    .tcbm-cta {
      display: block; line-height: 20px; padding: 12px 15px;
      font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .1em;
      max-width: 42vw; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
    }
  }
  /* At 360px the town tag is the first thing to go: the wordmark, the button
     and the burger are all load-bearing, the tag is a flourish. */
  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    .tcbm-town { display: none; }
